@startuml request-flow
!include _style.iuml

title Life of a request — https://track.caseyrquinn.com/workouts

actor "Browser" as client
participant "**edge-nginx**\n:80 / :443" as nginx #DCE9F7
participant "Docker DNS\n127.0.0.11" as dns #EEF0F3
participant "**grindtrack-app**\n:8080" as app #DFF0E3

autonumber "<b>[0]"

client -> client : DNS: track.caseyrquinn.com → VPS public IP
note right of client
  Every hostname here resolves to the **same** IP.
  Nothing in DNS says which app answers — that is
  decided entirely by the ""Host"" header / TLS SNI.
end note

== Plain HTTP on :80 is only ever a doorway ==

client -> nginx : GET http://track.caseyrquinn.com/workouts
activate nginx
alt path starts with /.well-known/acme-challenge/
  nginx --> client : 200 — file served from /var/www/certbot\n(this is how Let's Encrypt proves domain control)
else anything else
  nginx --> client : **301** → https://track.caseyrquinn.com/workouts
end
deactivate nginx

== TLS on :443 ==

client -> nginx : TCP connect :443, then TLS ClientHello\n**SNI = track.caseyrquinn.com**
activate nginx
note right of nginx
  SNI travels in the **clear**, before any encryption exists.
  It has to — nginx must know which certificate to present
  before it can decrypt anything.
end note
nginx -> nginx : match SNI against each ""server_name""\n→ track.conf's :443 block
nginx --> client : certificate for track.caseyrquinn.com\n(/etc/letsencrypt/live/track.../fullchain.pem)
client <-> nginx : finish handshake — TLS 1.2/1.3, session encrypted

== HTTP request, now inside TLS ==

client -> nginx : GET /workouts\nHost: track.caseyrquinn.com
nginx -> nginx : match ""Host"" against ""server_name"" again\n(SNI and Host are separate — both are checked)

nginx -> dns : resolve **grindtrack-app**
activate dns
dns --> nginx : 172.x.x.x  (cached for ""valid=30s"")
deactivate dns
note right of nginx
  This lookup happens **per request** because the upstream is
  held in a variable (""set $upstream""). A plain literal in
  ""proxy_pass"" would be resolved once at startup and pinned —
  an app redeploy would then 502 until nginx was reloaded.

  It resolves the **unique container name**, never the ""app""
  service alias: two stacks both register ""app"" on ""edge"",
  and Docker DNS would round-robin between them.
end note

nginx -> app : GET /workouts over the ""edge"" network\nHost, X-Real-IP, X-Forwarded-Proto: https\n**X-Forwarded-For: <client IP>  (overwritten)**
activate app
note right of nginx
  For grindtrack the XFF header is **set**, not appended.
  Its login rate limiter keys on the first entry, so if the
  client's own ""X-Forwarded-For"" were preserved, an attacker
  could inject a fake earlier hop and rotate past the limiter.
end note
app --> nginx : 200 + body
deactivate app
nginx --> client : 200, re-encrypted, plus HSTS /\nX-Frame-Options / nosniff headers
deactivate nginx

== The unmatched case ==

client -> nginx : request with Host/SNI matching nothing\n(direct-IP probe, spoofed Host, stale DNS)
activate nginx
nginx -> nginx : falls through to ""zz-default-drop.conf""\n(""default_server"", loaded last — ""zz-"" prefix)
nginx -->x client : **444** — connection closed, no response
deactivate nginx

@enduml